Q: Serialize a Static Class? A: <p>Another approach could be to use Singleton Pattern. You can have something like this (exception handling omitted for clarity):</p>
<pre><code>using System.Text.Json;

public class MyConfiguration
{
    #region Singleton    
    private static MyConfiguration _instance;
    public static MyConfiguration Instance {
        get {  
            if (_instance == null) _instance = new MyConfiguration();
            return _instance; 
        }
    }
    #endregion

    #region My settings
    public int SettingA { get; set; } = 10; // good to define initial defaults here
    public string SettingB { get; set; } = &quot;Hello&quot;; 
    #endregion

    #region Persistence
    public void Save() {
        File.WriteAllText(
            this.GetType().Name + &quot;.json&quot;, 
            JsonSerializer.Serialize(this, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true }));        
    }

    public void Load()
    {
        _instance = JsonSerializer.Deserialize&lt;MyConfiguration&gt;(
                File.ReadAllText(this.GetType().Name + &quot;.json&quot;));
    }    
    #endregion
}
</code></pre>
<p>The singleton pattern can be modified to be thread-safe, you can get the idea here: <a href="https://csharpindepth.com/articles/singleton" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Implementing the Singleton Pattern in C#</a></p>
<p>I find this elegant and super simple to use for variaous settings-classes:</p>
<pre><code>// almost as easy as MyConfiguration.SettingA ;-)
var myActualValue = MyConfiguration.Instance.SettingA;

// loading 
MyConfiguration.Instance.Load();
// saving
MyConfiguration.Instance.Save();
</code></pre>
<p>You spare messy property/field parsing.</p>
